Benchmark contamination in Large Language Models (LLMs) undermines evaluation validity, yet most detection methods require privileged access to training data, model internals, or output probabilities.
We present the first systematic characterization of Item Response Theory (IRT) person-fit statistics for contamination detection, operating at the minimal-information point from the target model: only binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns are required, combined with externally calibrated four-parameter logistic (4PL) item parameters; diagnostic $\theta$-anchoring additionally requires a clean baseline.
The core insight is psychometric: a contaminated LLM produces aberrant response patterns analogous to a test-taker with item preknowledge.
Using the standardized person-fit statistic $\ell_z^*$ in a difference-in-differences design ($\Delta\ell_z^*$), we identify four IRT-specific behavior boundaries through controlled experiments across three model families (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B; $n{=}10$ seeds each) on MMLU (14,042 items) and ARC-Challenge (295 items):
(1) a non-monotonic dose-response (observed between 15\% and 50\% contamination) where free-$\theta$ estimation attenuates detection at higher contamination ($|d|{=}6.58$ at 15\% vs.\ $|d|{=}1.90$ at 50\% for Qwen);
(2) model-specific $\theta$-shift dynamics linking baseline ability to detection sensitivity;
(3) a detection-diagnosis tradeoff where simpler statistics (accuracy $z$-scores, HT) yield stronger detection ($|d|$ several-fold to over an order of magnitude larger) but lack the diagnostic capabilities of $\ell_z^*$ (seen/unseen decomposition, mechanistic $\theta$-shift insight, sensitivity to response-pattern structure at 57/60 vs.\ 4/60 flagged cells);
and (4) a preliminary post-RL observation ($N{=}1$, single model and RL method) that GRPO erases the accuracy gap but $\ell_z^*$ response-pattern structure persists.
These findings clarify what binary response patterns can and cannot reveal about contamination, establishing boundary conditions for IRT-based detection.

